hi I'm Evan Carmichael I believe in  entrepreneurs and I also do a lot of  work helping big companies sell to small  businesses and I thought I'd create a  new series to help these big businesses  do a better job of selling the small  businesses so my first tip my first bit  of advice is stop saying small business  small businesses don't self-identify as  being a small business and I get how  it's easy in big companies to to  classify people right you have  enterprise medium business small  business it's easy to have a VP of small  business or a small business marketing  manager the problem is these small  businesses they don't call themselves  small businesses so you don't want to be  running that in your marketing your  campaigns your advertising because it  doesn't connect to people well I riesen  did a survey to my newsletter list which  has almost 80,000 people on it all  entrepreneur small businesses and I  asked them what do you consider yourself  to be and I gave them options  entrepreneur business owner small  business and here were the results 60.2  percent of people said entrepreneur 29.2  percent of people said business owner  six point six percent of people said  small business and four percent of  people chose a different answer so only  six point six percent of small  businesses actually self-identify as  being a small business now if you want  to have success in your marketing right  you've got to be able to speak in the  same language that people speak in I  call small businesses small business  when I'm talking to big companies when  I'm talking to brains because that's how  they call it internally but I never say  small business when I'm talking  entrepreneurs and the big businesses  have to do the same thing internally  maybe you still call it small business  but when you're out public facing  talking to entrepreneurs stop saying  small businesses because that's not how  they consider themselves to be now on  top of that a lot of entrepreneurs find  the term to be offensive not everybody  but a number of entrepreneurs find the  term small business to be offensive  here's part of what they said they'd  consider it to be  limiting degrading reflective of their  ability just because somebody is small  it doesn't mean that they are small  person or they have small goals and some  entrepreneurs not everybody but some of  them consider the fact that you're  calling them small they take that as a  personal insult so at best you're  calling people by something that they  don't associate with and at worst you're  offending them and that's definitely not  something you want to have in your  marketing your campaigns here is some of  the specific feedback that came out of  the survey I run a profitable regular  sized business I definitely don't think  that everything that required a lot of  my time thoughts and passion as small I  refuse to be degraded I consider myself  as an entrepreneur I'm a business owner  a one-man band but I'm not small I never  used the word small on issues relating  to my business I always use the word  entrepreneur the word small action  limits ones potential in my opinion  first and foremost I'm a business owner  and entrepreneur the problem is a  government to find small businesses as  50 employees or less and to me 50 is a  big business a business owner I'm in my  startup phase and it's just me on board  I'm still not a small business I always  refer to myself as a business owner on  the basis it is factually correct  irrespective of size status or any other  adjectives you care to mention  interesting question and yes being  labeled small can be frustrating  particularly so when you feel that is  being used to suggest your ability that  impact your chosen market might be  limited by size I struggled to find a  polite way to say this but i would  venture that in the context of  categorizing businesses the word small  is usually used by someone whose  abilities to exist beyond the  comfortable safety of an established  corporate structure might be seriously  limited by definition I'm an  entrepreneur it is something that has  bothered me for some time although I've  never articulated it I consider myself  to be a business owner if someone were  to count my immediate permanent staff  they could conclude that I was small  however if they also included the  specialized consultants who I work with  then I would be considered bigger if  they then consider the influence I have  to enable change in other organizations  and I could be considered a large  business  I guess it all depends upon whether one  sees size in terms of headcount or  influence and in the famous words of  marketing giant David Ogilvy I don't  know the rules of grammar but if you're  trying to persuade people to do  something or buy something it seems to  me you should use their language the  language they use everyday the language  in which they think we try to write in  the vernacular David Ogilvy so stop  calling us small businesses and start  speaking our language belief if you guys  like this video you want to learn more  about how I help brands you can check  out Evan Carmichael calm / brands or  subscribe to the channel thank you guys  so much for watching I'll see you soon  hi ever kill English here with mermaid  junkutv are you on Evans newsletter  subscription if not you should be I read  every single one and you should too ciao  you
 

